We ran into this on 4.3.2 and reproduced it, in case the details help with the priority.

Setup: processing.guarantee exactly_once_v2, one persistent (RocksDB, timestamped) global store, and a state.dir that is kept across restarts (a Kubernetes StatefulSet with a volume).

What we see: once the global store has flushed a memtable after it was opened, the "open" status in its offsets column family is on disk. A SIGKILL from then on (an OOM kill, or a kill after the shutdown grace period) leaves that status at 1, and every later start fails with TaskCorruptedException: "Tasks \[1-1\] are corrupted ... State store ... didn't find a valid state, since under EOS ... Invalid state during store open". The global thread dies and the "Detected unclean shutdown for global store" wipe in GlobalStateManagerImpl#initialize never runs, as described above. With at_least_once the same kill restarts fine, and so does a kill after a clean restart before the store's first flush. So for an exactly_once_v2 application with a persistent global store, a hard kill after that first flush ends in a crash loop until the global folder is deleted by hand.

